  },
  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper is present. Some tables and figures are flattened in the supplied text.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "Applies sparse autoencoder features to entity typing and NER in Gemma-2 models, an LLM setting, and reports results bearing on whether SAE features can recover information.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports entity-typing results, full-pipeline NER scores, and feature-ablation outcomes relevant to sparse SAE features in LLMs. Claims about toy-network geometry, capacity competition, and feature dimensionality are background or assumptions, not tested results. Silver-data perturbation and downstream training results do not test the representation claims.",
    "test_reason": "The paper uses pretrained Gemma Scope SAEs as a tool for zero-shot NER and reports only its own method's performance: entity-typing and NER F1 against MLP probes, plus ablations of the method's own feature-to-label map. It assumes SAE features are monosemantic. It does not measure superposition, feature spread across neurons, or whether SAE features are the model's units. Beating a probe on a downstream task does not test V4 or V5.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Broadly correct. The authors conclude that SAE features give a training-free signal for entity typing that beats trained probes, by up to +20 F1 in the biomedical domain. Full NER is high-precision but low-recall, limited mainly by heuristic span generation. Silver data helps only in cold-start settings, and false negatives are the main noise problem.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This is an application of pretrained Gemma-2 SAEs to NER, using feature-to-label precision mapping, entity-typing evaluation, and feature ablation; it does not reproduce the original toy-network superposition method or measure its geometry.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Entity-typing performance varies with layer, model size/type, domain, and SAE width. The paper states that layer 31 on Re-DocRED is below the probing baseline; it also reports different performance across IoU thresholds and span generators.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "With pretrained Gemma Scope SAEs, a training-free feature-to-label precision map beats dense MLP probes on entity typing in most configurations (AnEM 0.623 vs 0.421). It drops below the probe at layer 31 on Re-DocRED.",
        "proposed_claim": "SAE latents carry linearly usable entity-type information, and on downstream typing this can exceed dense probes, depending on layer.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R1",
          "R2",
          "R3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "For some fine-grained AnEM types, the feature mapping yielded an empty set of features. This gives zero recall for those classes.",
        "proposed_claim": "General-purpose SAE dictionaries may lack features for niche domain concepts. This is a possible incompleteness, but it is confounded by the mapping thresholds.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "Zeroing a class's mapped features cuts that class's typing F1 (TIME -0.58 at 50%), while random ablation changes it negligibly.",
        "proposed_claim": "Entity-type evidence in the method's classifier is spread over many SAE latents rather than a single one.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R20",
          "R21",
          "R22"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Joint Conference on Lexical and Computational Semantics (*SEM 2026), Proceedings of the 15th Joint Conference on Lexical and Computational Semantics",
    "problems": "The work is restricted to English and the Gemma-2 SAE family. Entity-typing results vary by layer and configuration; full NER also depends on heuristic span generation and has low recall, especially on AnEM. Feature-to-label mapping relies on gold annotations. The paper does not test feature stability, completeness, or canonical status.",
    "rows_left_out": "Table 1 precision and recall values are not separately cataloged because they are secondary to the reported F1 results and do not independently measure the representation claim. Silver-data noise experiments and downstream BERT training results are omitted because they test data quality and training effects rather than SAE representation properties.",
    "untested": [
      {
